WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:06.160
JAMIE DAVIDSON: Hi,
I'm Jamie Davidson.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.580
I run the product
team at Looker.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.205
And today I'm going to give you
a quick overview of what Looker

00:00:10.205 --> 00:00:13.760
is today and where we're going.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.280
Looker is an enterprise
BI tool and data platform.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.360
We empower our customers to
make a smarter use of data.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.170
On the top, you see the
diversity of use cases

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:30.720
the Looker platform can be
used to power from modern BI

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.770
and beyond.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.600
And I'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.590
Looker's platform has a
couple of key differences

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.960
relative to the existing market.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.040
Our customers come
with their data

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.010
aggregated from a wide
variety of sources

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.570
into a cloud data warehouse.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.320
Looker has an
in-database architecture

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.920
that will leverage the
functionality, scalability,

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.230
and performance of technologies
like BigQuery, and enable

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:06.410
customers to drive real
time data value from.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.420
We've got a semantic
modeling layer

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.390
on top of that in-database
architecture, where

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.860
an analyst can come and
codify their business metrics

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:21.170
and enable an organization to
have a single source of truth.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.830
That modeling layer is
integrated with Git inversion

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.980
control and allows
for governance.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.003
Looker is a cloud
native BI tool.

00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:34.420
And that means
that everything you

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:39.850
can do inside the application
and more is accessible via API.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.960
That architecture has a
couple of key benefits.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.340
First, data governance is built
in from the ground up, too.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.100
Because of the
modeling layer, because

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.950
of the integration with
Git, our security access

00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:00.110
and authentication, you can
have confidence that the metrics

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:03.230
that your users or
your business is using,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.670
or what you think
that they need,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.780
and that the right
people have access.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.600
That same semantic
modeling layer

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.580
also serves to empower
business users.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.570
You don't have to know the
complicated definitions

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.690
for things like gross
margin or lifetime value

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.680
to be able to still make
directional decisions based

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.400
on that data.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.440
Looker's a cloud
native application.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.520
Every URL is shareable.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.610
It means that you have
easy, repeatable analysis.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.130
Because we have an
in-database architecture,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:38.606
we are representing what's
live in the database today.

00:02:38.606 --> 00:02:44.320
An in-database architecture
allows for operational use

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:44.890
of your data.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.530
Because customers
are now replicating

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.380
their full transactional
information into a cloud data

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.805
warehouse, you can not just
look at the high level KPIs

00:02:54.805 --> 00:02:55.930
and where they're trending.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.140
You can actually see the
individual transactions

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.220
that are driving.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.670
Over 50% of our customers now
are actually using the Looker

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.570
platform beyond API context.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.310
To give you a
couple examples, we

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.360
see customers using us to
have embedded dashboards

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.170
in internal portals so that
their knowledge workers can use

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:19.890
data to make better decisions.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.810
We see customers embedding
dashboards into SAS tools,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.590
like Salesforce, so
that the salespeople

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.430
can go and have the relevant
information while they're

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.480
making their decisions.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.380
We see customers integrating
us into existing products,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.960
and even creating
completely new products

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.130
to give value to
their customers,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.080
and at times, monetize data.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.530
And then finally,
we see customers

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.530
going so far as to use Looker
effectively headlessly,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.640
so not using any of our UI or
UX, integrating with our APIs

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.340
to provide governed data
into custom applications.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.850
These applications don't
look or feel like anything

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:02.940
that classic BI would suggest.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.560
It's beyond reporting
and self-serve analytics.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.270
It's really operational.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:11.020
So for example, maybe a supply
chain management application

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:17.690
where the historical inventory
levels and pricing is useful,

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.839
and Looker provides a
governed view of that

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:22.210
into that application.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:23.630
So Looker is different.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.310
We have a different
architecture, too.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.960
And it gives some benefits.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.490
We are fast on any size of data.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.030
We're leveraging BigQuery,
and Snowflake, and Redshift

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.880
to be able to give
you real time access

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.340
to their powerful functionality.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.240
The semantic model
means that there's

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.780
a single source of truth
for your organization, too.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.640
And it gives the business
users trust in the data.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.140
That model is integrated with
the development workflow, too.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.200
It lets you have
changes over time

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.610
in a lifecycle of management
for those workflows.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.850
And finally, Looker is a
Cloud native application, too,

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.650
which means we've
got APIs to integrate

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.200
with lots of operational tools.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.480
We want to be where
the users are working.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.518
That was a quick look
at what Looker is today.

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:12.310
I want to tell you
about where we're going.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.180
We talked just recently about
the breadth of experiences

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.580
that we enable, or use
cases that we enable.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:23.230
We think about the ability to
serve those use cases in three

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.070
real layers, too.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.990
So the top layer is
data experiences.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.620
This is where the business users
are consuming data and taking

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:30.633
action on it.

00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:32.800
Below that, we've got our
application platform, too.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.950
This is what enables our
customers to create those data

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:36.950
experiences.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.670
And then finally, the
base, really, here

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:40.990
is Looker's foundation.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.937
This is our
development lifecycle.

00:05:43.937 --> 00:05:45.770
This is our ideas, our
query, our execution.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.040
This is the core of the app.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.570
So to start on data
experiences, I'm

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:54.787
going talk quickly about
the core unit of a report

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:55.870
for Looker as a dashboard.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.520
I'll talk about how
we've completely

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.180
rebuilt that dashboard stack,
and how we're similarly

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.090
now re-imagining that the
core explore experience,

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.930
that self-serve analysis is
the expression of the model

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:08.910
as well.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.410
We'll talk a little bit about
how we integrate into existing

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.080
business workflows, and
then finally close on

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.590
how we help with
content discovery.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:18.230
So I mentioned that we've
been rebuilding our dashboard.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:20.170
We've spent the
last year and a half

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.203
completely rebuilding
it from the ground up

00:06:22.203 --> 00:06:23.870
with a bunch of
different goals in mind.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:25.340
We want it to be a
more modern stack.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:26.590
We want to be more performant.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.730
We want it to be
more customizable.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:32.590
We also want to enable
fundamentally new interactions

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:36.060
and experiences that haven't
ever been possible inside BI,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.920
and that includes things
like cross-filtering

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.940
at petabyte scale.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:42.290
Because we have an
in-database architecture,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.580
and we can take advantage of
databases like the BigQuery,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.020
we now have an interaction
mechanism, too,

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.020
that lets you get full
granularity of your data.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.480
Ask as many questions as
you want of the content.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.750
We rethought the
consumption of content.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.950
We also had to think about the
creation of that content, too.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:02.220
We want to give our analysts
a whole lot more control

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.440
over the presentation
of their data, too.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.860
We want to make it easy
for them to create reports

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.440
that you can easily drive
insight from and ultimately

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.730
take action on.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.430
And that includes things
like fundamental new modes

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:15.900
of consumption.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.020
Here you can see
a new report type.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.710
This is closer to a Google
Doc than it is to a dashboard.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:25.505
It's still integrated with
the Looker governance.

00:07:25.505 --> 00:07:27.630
It's still integrated with
our security model, too.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.750
You can see it's
infused with data.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.900
It's got a live look and
visualization of metrics, too.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:38.430
But it's presented
in context, too.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:43.290
Imagine you're an executive
consuming weekly PDF

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.000
report emailed to you, too.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.440
The analyst needs
to construct it

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.830
in such a way where you can
easily derive insights from it,

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:49.330
too.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:49.980
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.955
So this can allow that.

00:07:51.955 --> 00:07:53.580
We're also increasing
our customization

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.790
as possible inside
dashboards, too,

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:57.840
with new dashboard
extensions, where

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.790
you can take over a
tile of a dashboard,

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.520
too, with pre-built
extensions that

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.378
are present inside
our marketplace,

00:08:04.378 --> 00:08:06.420
as well as custom extensions
that you build, too,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.470
to allow you to create custom
experiences and workflows

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.580
to tailor to your data.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.420
We're really excited about
bringing Looker more broadly

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.460
to as many people as possible.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.460
That includes doing
work like localization.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.700
We now support 23
different languages, too,

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.740
for our core
reporting use cases.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.540
We are in the
process of bringing

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.400
all of that localization
to the full application.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.760
Similarly, we're looking
at accessibility as well.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:34.990
We want to make
it easy for Looker

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.990
to navigate for all users,
too, so that everyone

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:39.450
can derive value from it.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.870
We're looking now
at exploration, too.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:46.410
And Looker's explorer has always
been an incredibly powerful

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.440
feature.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.100
It's the manifestation
of that semantic model.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:54.660
It lets you ask lots of
questions of your data, too.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.410
We want to make it even easier.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.200
We want to make it so you
have smarter starting points,

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.180
so pre-built queries to start
the question and answering

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.240
process.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.715
We want to make it so that you
can do on-the-fly calculations,

00:09:07.715 --> 00:09:09.090
and not have to
go into the model

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.760
to create new metrics
or dimensions, too,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.440
that are just transformations
on existing ones.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:17.746
We also want to take advantage
of GCP's best-in-class AI

00:09:17.746 --> 00:09:20.640
and ML functionality, too,
and integrate some of that

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.770
into the BI tool so
that you'd have things

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:26.430
like time series
forecasting to integrate it

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.250
into the explored experience.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.770
We want to bring
data to wherever work

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.540
is being done at a high level.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.550
We want to drive more value.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:39.170
That requires things like
our learning functionality.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:42.670
We've got analytical alerts that
use data to power workflows.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.260
We want to make
them even smarter.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:48.580
We want to use that same AI and
ML expertise that GCP has, too,

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.520
do things like outlier
detection, where analysts

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.950
can come in and say, this
metric is important to me.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.870
Let me know if it changes,
and maybe tell me why.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:57.495
We can do that.

00:09:57.495 --> 00:09:59.203
We'll be bringing
that into [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:59.203 --> 00:10:01.240
We want to make it easy
to take action directly

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.110
from within the BI experience.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.720
So this is building
on our action

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.100
help, which is
tightly integrated

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.020
with our semantic model.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:10.720
It allows for interaction
and integration

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.020
with third-party services.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.040
So in this case, you could
have complex analysis

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.470
inside the BI tool to find
over-performing campaigns, too,

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.850
and then take direct action
to change their budgets

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:27.880
or change activity inside
the Google Ads platform.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.350
We recently released an
integration with Google Sheets.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:31.990
We're really excited.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:35.620
Customers have adopted
it incredibly quickly,

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:38.080
because they wanted
to take governed,

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.335
trusted data, too,
into a context

00:10:40.335 --> 00:10:41.710
sometimes where
they want to have

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.948
some lightweight ad hoc analysis
to customize experience.

00:10:44.948 --> 00:10:46.490
We're excited about
this integration.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:47.860
We want to bring it forward.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:49.750
We want to do a
bi-directional integration

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.790
where Google Sheets
can basically

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:54.130
pull Looker data
and Looker consent

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:55.660
data into Google Sheets.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:58.810
We're also excited about
integrating with the broader G

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:00.010
Suite products.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:04.000
We've got lots of customers
that create slideshows that

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.970
are weekly operational
reports, where they

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:09.220
go through a process of
downloading a bunch of images,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:13.250
and reconstituting that
slideshow or deck every week.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.580
We want to make it so
that you have live access

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:18.160
to governed data, in the
context where you're working,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.180
too, to enable that data
storytelling within slides,

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:24.670
or similarly within Google Docs.

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:26.590
We've got a deep
integration with Slack,

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:28.540
where you can send
data from Looker

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:31.330
into Slack and request
data of Looker from Slack.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:35.530
We want to make that interface
of requesting data even easier

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.510
in that communications
context, too,

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:39.910
extending to a natural
language interface

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.380
to that semantic model.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:45.070
We're really excited about
a new native mobile app

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:48.280
experience we've got for IOS
and Android in beta today,

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.290
and we'll be GAing
over the course

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.020
of the second half of
this year, where you can

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.870
consume your data on the go.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:58.480
We also want to expose that
natural language interface

00:11:58.480 --> 00:11:59.830
in this context.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:03.170
It's not enough to have your
content inside your instance,

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:03.670
though.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.465
If you can't find it,
it's not useful, too.

00:12:05.465 --> 00:12:07.090
And we're really
excited now that we're

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.160
part of Google,
the search company,

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:13.000
too, to really invest heavily
in that content discovery.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.600
We're rebuilding our
search infrastructure

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.040
for a couple of goals--

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:20.350
better performance, better
relevance-- but also

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.980
with an eye to help with some
of the fundamental problems

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:26.020
that the BI always has.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.562
If you don't have
a piece of content,

00:12:27.562 --> 00:12:30.062
say you're searching for annual
recurring revenue, you don't

00:12:30.062 --> 00:12:31.910
have a piece of content
that's relevant,

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:33.970
we can automatically
generate that content

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:37.150
to give you a live look at your
total ARR inside that search

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:38.290
context, too.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.360
The data experiences
side, we are

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.940
focused on reporting
and self-serve analysis,

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.190
integrating with your workflow,
and driving more value

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:46.050
from your data.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.550
I'm going to switch gears now
and talk about our application

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:49.460
platform.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:52.110
This is where
customers and partners

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:53.960
build those data experiences.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:56.410
And we're very focused
here on a couple of things.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.360
We want to help accelerate
value for our customers.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.870
We want to increase
both the customizability

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:05.410
and power that's possible
inside the embedded context,

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.390
and also extend the platform.

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.330
To accelerate value, we've
released a marketplace

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:13.030
that's in beta currently,
where you can easily

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:18.240
find, install, and manage
pre-built pieces of content.

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:19.570
So these can be applications.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:21.310
These can be LookML blocks.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:22.510
These can be data sets.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.880
These can be custom
visualizations.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:28.030
And they will dramatically
accelerate your ability

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.450
to actually explore.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:31.570
One of those contexts and
one of those data sets,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.100
too, is Google Marketing.

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.587
We've had a application and
a series of LookML blocks,

00:13:36.587 --> 00:13:38.920
too, that are targeted on
marketing, [? in this case. ?]

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:42.690
We're really excited to
leverage the expertise in Google

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.670
to push this, really,
a whole lot further.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.520
We want to help our
customers be able to gather

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:51.163
all of their marketing
data together to analyze

00:13:51.163 --> 00:13:53.080
and find insights from
it, too, and ultimately

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.940
take action on that data,
too, within the BI experience.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.130
For embedding, we think that
there's a spectrum, actually,

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:02.320
of need for customization.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:04.505
And it sort of goes
from, on one side,

00:14:04.505 --> 00:14:06.130
there's inside the
BI tool, and there's

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.940
customization that's possible
inside a dashboard experience,

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:10.690
or inside an embedded
dashboard experience

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:12.640
where you have
themes so that it can

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:14.590
feel like the application
it's embedded in.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:16.840
We want to increase the
amount of customization that's

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:18.700
possible in that context.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:22.130
Similarly, we've got our APIs on
the other end of the spectrum,

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:22.630
too.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:25.430
We want to both increase the
customization as possible,

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:27.670
but also give you more
out of the box there, too.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.870
So we want richer
APIs, better SDKs

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.190
for interfacing with
those APIs, too,

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.800
and then even high-level
JavaScript-based components,

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:38.710
too, so it's much easier to
be able to create [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:41.248
workflows to customize.

00:14:41.248 --> 00:14:42.790
So as an example,
here is a dashboard

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.582
that's been themed so
that it can basically

00:14:44.582 --> 00:14:48.420
fit into an application,
too, and feel native.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:52.090
We very literally took our
new dashboard code base, too,

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.940
and packaged up the new
functionality and features,

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.730
too, in a JavaScript
component SDK

00:14:57.730 --> 00:14:59.830
so that customers
don't have to recreate

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.790
filters or visualizations and
integrate them with our APIs.

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:06.160
They can, instead,
take our visualizations

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.840
and extend them,
and customize them

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.150
in any way that might fit
their specific workflow.

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:13.120
We're investing heavily
in our APIs and SDKs, too,

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:19.230
from documentation to more
robust interactions as well.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:21.710
And we're also focused
on extending what's

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:23.380
possible inside the platform.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:26.060
I'm really excited to talk
about the extension framework.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:27.980
We've got a bunch of
partners and customers

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:31.040
that are excited about
the potential here.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.990
The extension framework
is the ability

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.500
to host custom JavaScript
in a sandboxed environment

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:40.250
within your Looker instance,
too, and have direct access

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:43.250
to Looker's API so
that you can leverage

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.450
security and governance.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.485
And it's a part of
the Looker platform.

00:15:48.485 --> 00:15:50.610
There are lots of different
potential applications.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:52.027
I'll give you a
couple of examples

00:15:52.027 --> 00:15:54.990
to show what's possible.

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:58.360
We've released our
first extension--

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.630
I mean, our marketplace,
too, and our data dictionary.

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:04.380
This is closely integrated
with our data model APIs, too.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.220
It can show analysts
and developers

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.940
the data that's present and
the definitions behind them

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.162
in a simple, easy place.

00:16:14.162 --> 00:16:15.870
We've got a lot of
partners and customers

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.730
that are excited
about creating custom

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:20.490
ways to explore their data.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:25.740
Going beyond the pivot table
metaphor of the Explore page,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.990
there are contexts to where
a more tailored experience

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:28.865
actually makes sense.

00:16:28.865 --> 00:16:31.270
So in this example,
the cohort tool,

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.450
it's a very
parameterized workflow,

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.420
where you select a measure, you
select an aggregation place,

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:40.740
you select the interval that
you want to measure cohorts on,

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:44.040
as opposed to needing
the full canvas

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.360
of [INAUDIBLE] pivot table.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:49.620
Another example is actually
a hosted notebook experience,

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:52.650
where you can leverage
the power of Python,

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:54.920
integrated with the
Looker API, too,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:59.280
and go beyond what a classic
Explorer experience would be.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.860
Really excited about the
application platform.

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.033
We're focused on increasing
the customization

00:17:04.033 --> 00:17:06.450
inside the embedded experience,
increasing the performance

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:09.099
there, too, and accelerating
value for our customers.

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:10.516
I'm going to switch
gears and talk

00:17:10.516 --> 00:17:13.345
about the foundational
core of Looker.

00:17:13.345 --> 00:17:15.720
Here, we think about things
like our software development

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.359
lifecycle and IDE for
the data developers.

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:19.484
We think about performance.

00:17:19.484 --> 00:17:21.109
We think about
transformations that can

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:22.410
power that performance, too.

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:24.890
And how can we leverage
the data model?

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.540
Looker's always been
tightly integrated

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.230
with a development workflow.

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:30.470
And we want to continue
to push that forward.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:34.140
We want to have a full quality
assurance process, too,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.460
in a single instance that
allows for development, staging,

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:42.090
and verification of development
and production environments.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.980
And we want that to be
inclusive of your LookML models,

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:49.760
of your content, and even your
transformations in database,

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:50.480
too.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.670
It's in the service of
driving some performance.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:55.880
And Looker's
in-database architecture

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:59.600
gives us the ability to
actually drive performance

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:01.850
in a layered approach.

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:04.570
We can take advantage of
best-in-class functionality

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:05.570
of data warehouses, too.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:09.680
And they also use BI contexts
to help inform queries

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:11.572
and drive more
performance queries.

00:18:11.572 --> 00:18:13.280
We've got a much
customers really excited

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:14.363
about aggregate awareness.

00:18:14.363 --> 00:18:18.080
We released it a few months ago,
where you can create roll up

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:19.280
tables within Looker.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:22.190
And Looker's platform
will have the intelligence

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:24.830
to be able to dynamically
optimize queries based

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:29.820
on the questions or the
query that's coming in.

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.610
So if you're going for a
granular piece of information,

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:34.670
we can go to the
full granular table.

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:37.020
If you're looking
for a summary, you

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:39.170
can go to the high
level [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:40.850
And to power that
aggregate awareness,

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:43.183
too, we've been investing
heavily in our transformation,

00:18:43.183 --> 00:18:46.550
so making them much more
robust and more durable.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:49.730
We're focused on adding new
functionality, like the ability

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:52.700
to actually incrementally load
into your aggregate tables,

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.820
and integrating those
transformations [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:58.550
We're taking a layered
approach to performance

00:18:58.550 --> 00:18:59.290
and acceleration.

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:01.790
We want to take advantage of
the best-in-class functionality

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:05.340
like BigQuery's BI engine
inside the database.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.890
It's an in-database cache,
specifically targeting

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:09.990
BI use cases.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:13.490
Similarly, we also have
a new in-memory cache

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.950
as well inside the
look application layer

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:19.430
that eliminates the need to do
round-trips to the database,

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:22.788
too, when they're not necessary.

00:19:22.788 --> 00:19:25.080
We think about administration
a little bit differently.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.515
We think about it from an
analytics-first perspective.

00:19:27.515 --> 00:19:29.140
We want to give you
as much information

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.360
about the usage of
your Looker platform

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:34.480
to help you drive
data-driven decisions.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.585
And so you can use the
actual query performance

00:19:38.585 --> 00:19:40.210
to help drive
performance optimization.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:41.980
You can use the
actual user engagement

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.650
to help better
captivate your audience.

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:48.890
Looker's data model
is the key reason

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:50.390
why our BI tools differentiate.

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:52.140
It's that integration
with the data model,

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.170
and understanding of the
transformational logic

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:55.610
that's present inside.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:57.620
We have a bunch of
customers now using

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:01.700
that transformational logic in
other workflows or use cases

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:02.840
outside of Looker.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:04.670
So we've got a bunch
of customers now using

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:07.610
stable roll-up tables,
too, inside the database

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.110
to power machine
learning workflows,

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:12.530
where they've got governed
definitions of features

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:14.915
for their machine learning
training processes,

00:20:14.915 --> 00:20:16.790
too, that are
governed by Looker,

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:18.470
but used in other contexts.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:23.180
Similarly, we also want to help
further leverage the Looker

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:26.630
model, too, like we're doing
inside the Google Sheets

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:27.140
integration.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.000
We want to basically
let analysts

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.260
use the right analytical tools,
too, on top of governed data.

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:36.428
We think we create more value
when you give governed trust

00:20:36.428 --> 00:20:38.720
and access to that data, too,
regardless of the context

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.580
where it's being consumed.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:43.880
Looker and GCP firmly
believes that customers

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:44.922
have the right to choose.

00:20:44.922 --> 00:20:47.172
They have the right to choose
the right infrastructure

00:20:47.172 --> 00:20:48.320
for the right problem.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.000
We firmly support that, too.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:54.410
We currently support hosting
Looker inside GCP, inside AWS,

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:56.930
and we're actively working
on supporting Azure.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.810
We are also very
focused on security.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:03.110
It's a large effort inside our
engineering and product teams.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.390
We've already got our
[INAUDIBLE] type 2.

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:10.880
We've already got our ISO
27K, and our PCI [INAUDIBLE],,

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:11.930
as well.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.450
We're actively working
on additional security

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:16.960
certifications, too.

